    "# Extended Bayesian Model v2 — Nested Hierarchical with Physical-Geographical Covariate\n",
    "\n",
    "Model Objective: Advanced estimation and simulation of river flows ($Q$) incorporating a three-level hierarchical structure and a physical-geographical covariate — the catchment area ($\\text{catchment\\_area}$). The model eliminates the key pathology of the baseline model: the instability of local scale parameters $\\sigma_s$ resulting from learning exclusively on data from a single baseline year.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1. Variables and Data Preparation\n",
    "\n",
    "The data structure is based on a cascading relationship mapping observations to stations, and stations to specific rivers. Input data filtration includes replacing zero values with a safe value of $0.1 \\text{ m}^3/\\text{s}$ to ensure numerical stability during the logarithmic transformation.\n",
    "\n",
    "* **$N$** – Total number of observations (daily measurements) in the dataset ($N > 22\\ 000$).\n",
    "* **$S$** – Number of unique gauging stations ($S = 69$).\n",
    "* **$R$** – Number of unique rivers grouping the gauging stations.\n",
    "* **$y_n$** – Observed daily river flow for observation $n$ [$\\text{m}^3/\\text{s}$].\n",
    "* **$\\text{station\\_id}[n]$** – Vector mapping observation $n$ to a specific station $s \\in \\{1, \\dots, S\\}$.\n",
    "* **$\\text{river\\_id}[s]$** – Vector mapping station $s$ to its parent river $r \\in \\{1, \\dots, R\\}$.\n",
    "* **$\\widetilde{c}_s$** – Standardized logarithm of the catchment area for station $s$ [$\\text{km}^2$], calculated as $\\widetilde{c}_s = \\frac{\\log(\\text{catchment}_s) - \\overline{\\log c}}{\\text{sd}(\\log c)}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "The indexing path flows in a cascade: observation $n$ belongs to station $s[n]$, which in turn belongs to river $r[s]$, denoted for brevity as $r[s[n]]$.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 2. Mathematical Architecture\n",
    "\n",
    "The model utilizes a **hybrid (mixed) architecture** identical to version v1, with a crucial extension: the scale parameter $\\log\\sigma_s$ is now modeled as a linear function of a stable physical-geographical predictor.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Covariate Justification\n",
    "\n",
    "The catchment area is **constant over time** — it does not change between the baseline year (2023) and the validation year (2024). Stations with a large catchment area (e.g., TCZEW, 194,000 km²) naturally exhibit higher flow variability than small mountain streams (e.g., MYSZYNIEC, 100 km²). Including this feature as a predictor decouples the estimation of $\\sigma_s$ from the anomalies of a single baseline year — directly addressing the pathology of the MYSZYNIEC station (threshold 13× above the median) and PTAKI (28.96% false alarms).\n",
    "\n",
    "### Model Equations:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Likelihood Function:**\n",
    "$$y_n \\sim \\text{LogNormal}\\!\\left(\\mu_{s[n]},\\ \\sigma_{s[n]}\\right)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Station Level — $\\mu_s$ (Centered Parameterization):**\n",
    "$$\\mu_s \\sim \\mathcal{N}\\!\\left(\\mu_{r[s]},\\ \\tau_{\\mu,\\,\\text{sta}}\\right)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Station Level — $\\sigma_s$ with Physical-Geographical Covariate:**\n",
    "\n",
    "The logarithm of the station noise scale is the sum of three components: the parent river profile, the catchment area effect, and the residual station variability:\n",
    "$$\\log\\sigma_s = \\underbrace{\\log\\sigma_{r[s]}}_{\\text{river profile}}\n",
    "  + \\underbrace{\\alpha_c \\cdot \\widetilde{c}_s}_{\\text{catchment effect}}\n",
    "  + \\underbrace{\\tau_{\\sigma,\\,\\text{sta}} \\cdot \\tilde{\\varepsilon}_s}_{\\text{station noise}}$$\n",
    "$$\\tilde{\\varepsilon}_s \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, 1), \\qquad \\sigma_s = \\exp(\\log\\sigma_s)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "4. **River Level — Non-Centered Parameterization:**\n",
    "$$\\mu_r = \\mu_{\\text{global}} + \\tau_{\\mu,\\,\\text{riv}} \\cdot z_{\\mu,r}, \\qquad z_{\\mu,r} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)$$\n",
    "$$\\log\\sigma_r = \\log\\sigma_{\\text{global}} + \\tau_{\\sigma,\\,\\text{riv}} \\cdot z_{\\sigma,r}, \\qquad z_{\\sigma,r} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 3. Prior Distributions and Sampling Configuration\n",
    "\n",
    "| Parameter | Distribution | Scope and role in the hierarchy |\n",
    "| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n",
    "| $\\mu_{\\text{global}}$ | $\\mathcal{N}(3,\\ 1.2)$ | Global reference point for log-flows on a national scale. |\n",
    "| $\\log\\sigma_{\\text{global}}$ | $\\mathcal{N}(0,\\ 0.5)$ | Central baseline value for the noise scale. |\n",
    "| $\\alpha_c$ | $\\mathcal{N}(0,\\ 0.5)$ | Effect of the catchment area on the variability scale. (If positive $\\alpha_c$, a larger catchment → greater variability). |\n",
    "| $\\tau_{\\mu,\\,\\text{riv}}$ | $\\text{Half-}\\mathcal{N}(0,\\ 1.2)$ | Scale of variability for mean flows between rivers. |\n",
    "| $\\tau_{\\sigma,\\,\\text{riv}}$ | $\\text{Half-}\\mathcal{N}(0,\\ 0.5)$ | Scale of variability for flow dynamics between rivers. |\n",
    "| $\\tau_{\\mu,\\,\\text{sta}}$ | $\\text{Half-}\\mathcal{N}(0,\\ 1.2)$ | Local variability of station means within the same river. |\n",
    "| $\\tau_{\\sigma,\\,\\text{sta}}$ | $\\text{Half-}\\mathcal{N}(0,\\ 0.5)$ | Residual station noise variability after accounting for the catchment area. |\n",
    "| $z_{\\mu,r},\\ z_{\\sigma,r}$ | $\\mathcal{N}(0,\\ 1)$ | Standardized deviations at the river level (NCP). |\n",
    "| $\\tilde{\\varepsilon}_s$ | $\\mathcal{N}(0,\\ 1)$ | Standardized station residual for $\\sigma_s$ (NCP). |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Sampler Control Parameters (MCMC)\n",
    "\n",
    "* `adapt_delta = 0.99` – decreased integration step size to minimize divergence\n",
    "* `max_treedepth = 15` – increased search tree depth\n"
