    "### 2024 Out-of-Sample Validation and Model Comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "The out-of-sample validation on the 2024 hydrological dataset provides a rigorous test of the operational performance of both frameworks. Across a total of **22,203 analyzed station-days**, the macro-level detection rates remain remarkably consistent between the two model configurations:\n",
    "\n",
    "* **95% Anomaly Threshold:** Model v2 flagged **1,837 days (8.27%)** compared to the Baseline's **1,835 days (8.26%)**.\n",
    "* **99% Extreme Threshold:** Model v2 flagged **348 days (1.57%)** compared to the Baseline's **347 days (1.56%)**.\n",
    "* **Zero-Extreme Stations:** Both models left exactly **27 stations** with 0% extreme days, indicating an identical spatial footprint regarding severe flood alerts.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### Key Operational Insights\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 1. Global Stability vs. Local Shrinkage\n",
    "The inclusion of the physical-geographical covariate ($\\alpha_c = -0.119$) did not cause a widespread shift in the operational alarm infrastructure. At the national scale, the aggregate likelihood and group-level priors tightly constrain the parameters, rendering the macroscopic detection rates virtually identical. The physical covariate acts as a fine-tuning mechanism for variance allocation rather than a structural overhaul.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 2. Resolution of Pathological Stations\n",
    "An analysis of the historically problematic stations confirms that the regional parameters are highly robust. For instance:\n",
    "* **PTAKI & TUCHOLA:** Maintained identical 95% thresholds ($20.2~\\mathrm{m^3/s}$ and $26.0~\\mathrm{m^3/s}$ respectively) and unvaried alarm rates ($28.96\\%$ and $16.39\\%$).\n",
    "* **LOWICZ & WLODAWA:** Exhibited minute threshold shifts (e.g., Łowicz adjusted from $38.3$ to $38.4~\\mathrm{m^3/s}$), but these numeric refinements yielded a $0.00\\%$ net change in out-of-sample alarm triggering.\n",
    "\n",
    "This stability indicates that the data-driven pooling provided by the massive number of daily observations ($N \\gg 300$ per station) naturally dominates the hierarchical adjustments, preventing the model from over-adjusting to the new geographic covariate.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 3. Persistent Localized Volatility\n",
    "The top 10 stations with the highest anomaly counts (led by **PTAKI** at $28.96\\%$ and **DOBRYLAS** at $27.21\\%$) demonstrate that specific catchments experience sustained deviations from the theoretical log-normal core. Because these high flag rates persist across both model variants, they point toward unmodeled local hydraulic factors—such as seasonal reservoir management, artificial flow regulations, or complex flood-plain geometries—rather than a miscalibration of the regional variance scales."
